{
  "dom_id": "step-e3734",
  "name": "Example: Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points",
  "problem": "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/aa8ee7171576bc92bb9b6a6730223e2629bc0d6216cb54a7a8a9f51d97985d14.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f'(2.5) < f'(1) < f'(-1)$\n\n- $f'(-1) < f'(1) < f'(2.5)$\n\n- $f'(1) < f'(-1) < f'(2.5)$",
  "worked_solution": "From the graph, we see that\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so $f'(-1) > 0.$\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so $f'(1) = 0.$\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=2.5$ is negative, and so $f'(2.5) < 0.$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is correct.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
